{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791580371,"nonce":"SnSnnhbAnrCE6Nuam36PLcm6vwg=","title":"Type ids derived from symbol ids collide with real type ids (TypeId::from_raw(symbol.as_raw()))","message":"tast_to_hir registers class/interface/enum/abstract/alias declarations under `TypeId::from_raw(symbol.as_raw())`, and MIR (construction.rs, decl/signatures.rs) derives fallback type ids the same way. Those values share one integer range with real TypeIds, and maps such as `constructor_map` mix both. Which pairs collide depends on how many symbols and types a context created, so it moves with compilation order and with the cache.\n\nSeen: reserving SymbolId(0) (shifting every symbol id by one) made `super()` in Issue6446's CustomStringBuf bind to haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator.new instead of StringBuf.new: the parent's symbol-derived TypeId landed on ArrayIterator's real one in constructor_map. That change was reverted (ef2f94f fixed at the `this` use site instead), but the hazard exists at HEAD with today's numbering too: any shift in symbol creation order can rebind a constructor, and the cached and uncached paths number differently.\n\nFix direction: key HIR declarations and constructor_map by SymbolId (or a dedicated newtype), never a TypeId minted from a symbol.","files":null}]}